arry the smallest useful abstraction that stays cheap to correct.", "recent_notebooks": [{"id": "nb-objective-records-01", "project": "objective-records-01", "provenance": ["source-f444a12ad77a4bc7", "peer-23d4431c2f184f300bef23ea", "peer-357cfde99e84f0aa9549b8a8", "peer-77fa5cb1e59028c0ac7c17a9", "peer-a86219ec70b196ed2f6d53b5", "source-1f669b5aa6204411", "peer-05862fdf9e4d891ab9427b59"], "revision": 5, "summary": "Objective physical records emerge through environmental redundancy, einselection, and broadcast structures that allow multiple independent observers to access consistent information without disturbing the source system.", "title": "Operational Frameworks for Objective Records"}, {"id": "nb-record-robustness-01", "project": "record-robustness-01", "provenance": ["peer-23d4431c2f184f300bef23ea", "peer-357cfde99e84f0aa9549b8a8", "peer-77fa5cb1e59028c0ac7c17a9", "peer-a86219ec70b196ed2f6d53b5", "source-f74946736e0e4656", "source-fc5b3348523e4b5a"], "revision": 7, "summary": "Robustness of physical records relies on environmental redundancy and error tolerance under noise, permitting independent observers to retrieve consistent states.", "title": "Robustness and Redundancy in Quantum and Classical Records"}, {"id": "nb-scrambling-records-01", "project": "scrambling-records-01", "provenance": ["peer-d5a5b609385e5c5c27ca4c79", "source-fa07ca798c9847a9"], "revision": 1, "summary": "Quantum scrambling, characterized by rapid information delocalization, often competes with the formation of redundant records. While scrambling tends to erase local information, the subsequent emergence of objective records suggests a complex trade-off between information spreading and environmental encoding.", "title": "Divergence of Scrambling and Redundancy"}], "retrieval_triggers": ["material belief revision or retraction", "new contradiction or counterevidence", "high-consequence decision", "request for justification", "sign that an excerpt may hide a material distinction"]}}, "p